<title>BELGIUM: Commission proposes renewed EIB lending ceilings.</title>
<headline>Commission proposes renewed EIB lending ceilings.</headline>
<dateline>BRUSSELS 1996-11-13</dateline>
<text>
<p>The European Commission on Wednesday adopted proposals for new mandates for European Investment Bank lending to third countries amounting to 6,825 million Ecus during the 1997-99 period.</p>
<p>The proposal represents an increase of about 12.5 percent for the regions concerned, with a large proportion targeted for central and eastern Europe.</p>
<p>The Commission's plan is based on the assumption that loan guarantees from the EU's budget will in the future provide 65 percent coverage.</p>
<p>The Commission said this is consistent with the conclusions of the ECOFIN on October 14 which envisaged guarantees in the range of 65 to 75 percent.</p>
<p>The new mandates will be contained in a Council decision adopted under Article 235.</p>
<p>Following is the regional breakdown for new lending.</p>
<p>    Central and Eastern Europe	   3,450 Ecus million</p>
<p>    Mediterranean countries		2,100</p>
<p>    Latin America and Asia		   900</p>
<p>    South Africa				 375</p>
</text>
